  "ai_overview": {
    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Yes, standard homeowners insurance policies in Utah typically cover hail damage. This protection falls under dwelling coverage (Coverage A) for your roof, siding, and structure, as well as other structures coverage for items like detached garages or sheds.",
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1,
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Aspects of Hail Coverage in Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Deductibles: Many Utah policies use a separate percentage-based deductible for wind and hail damage\u2014often 1% to 2% of your dwelling coverage amount\u2014rather than a flat dollar amount.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Actual Cash Value (ACV) vs. Replacement Cost Value (RCV): RCV policies pay the full cost to replace your damaged roof or materials without subtracting for age, whereas ACV policies factor in depreciation based on the roof's age.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Cosmetic Exclusions: Some modern policies include a \"cosmetic damage exclusion,\" meaning the insurer will not pay for dents or marks that affect only the appearance of metal roofs, siding, or garage doors without impairing their actual function.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Utah Uniformity Rule: Under state guidelines, if repair or replacement leaves your home with a mismatched appearance (such as replacement siding or shingles that do not match the old ones), the insurer may be required to cover a broader replacement to restore a reasonably uniform look.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "What to Check in Your Policy"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Look for a separate wind/hail deductible to see what out-of-pocket costs apply before your insurance pays out.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Check the filing time limit, as most Utah policies require you to report storm damage within 1 to 2 years of the event.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              8
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Review whether your roof is covered by ACV or RCV.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "If you'd like, share:"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Your insurance provider or policy type"
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Whether you are dealing with a recent storm"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "I can help you understand what steps to take next or how to prepare for an inspection."
      }
    ],
